<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="notes">
        <section xml:id="notes-week-06">
            <title>Gerrymandering Introduction</title>

  <p>
    These notes follow the structure of a public lecture (by Prof. Gibbons) on the mathematics of gerrymandering,
    adapted for our class introduction to the topic.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="what-is-gerrymandering">
    <title>What Is Gerrymandering?</title>

    <definition>
      <term>Gerrymander (verb)</term>
      <statement>
        To manipulate the boundaries of an electoral constituency so as to favor
        one party or class.
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Why is a mathematician talking about this?
    </p>

    <p>
      The American Mathematical Society (AMS) and the American Statistical Association (ASA)
      have issued statements noting:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Existing districting requirements do not prevent partisan gerrymandering.</li>
      <li>Modern data and computing make partisan manipulation easier.</li>
      <li>Mathematical and statistical tools can detect unfair advantage.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Our goal: understand what <q>fair</q> might mean — and how mathematics helps test it.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="tiny-state">
    <title>A Tiny State Example</title>

    <p>
      Suppose a state has 50 voters and 5 districts.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>30 voters (60%) prefer Blue.</li>
      <li>20 voters (40%) prefer Red.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Depending on how we draw district lines, we can get very different outcomes.
    </p>

    <example>
      <title>Possible Outcomes</title>

      <ul>
        <li>1 Blue, 4 Red (<q>Anything Goes</q>)</li>
        <li>3 Blue, 2 Red (Proportional Representation)</li>
        <li>5 Blue, 0 Red (Blue Gerrymander)</li>
        <li>2 Blue, 3 Red (Red Gerrymander)</li>
      </ul>
    </example>

    <p>
      Same voters. Same preferences. Different district lines.
    </p>

    <p>
      Question: what should <q>fair</q> mean here?
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="social-criteria">
    <title>Social and Legal Criteria for Fairness</title>

    <p>
      Federal requirement (Reynolds v. Sims, 1964):
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      The Equal Protection Clause demands substantially equal state legislative
      representation for all citizens.
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      In practice: districts must have roughly equal population (often within 10%).
    </p>

    <p>
      Common state-level criteria:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Contiguity (districts must be connected).</li>
      <li>Compactness (residents should live reasonably close together).</li>
      <li>Communities of interest.</li>
      <li>Respect for political boundaries (don’t split towns/counties).</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Notice: none of these directly prevent partisan bias.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="mathematical-criteria">
    <title>Mathematical Criteria for Partisan Fairness</title>

    <ul>
      <li>Proportionality</li>
      <li>Convexity and Compactness</li>
      <li>Efficiency Gap</li>
      <li>Sampling Methods</li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="proportionality">
    <title>Proportionality</title>

    <p>
      If 67% of voters prefer Party X, then roughly 67% of seats
      should go to Party X.
    </p>

    <p>
      In the tiny state example:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>60% Blue → 3 out of 5 seats seems reasonable.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      But proportionality is not guaranteed in single-member districts.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="convexity">
    <title>Convexity and Compactness</title>

    <p>
      Hope: Convex, compact maps are harder to gerrymander.
    </p>

    <p>
      Reality: You can gerrymander using convex districts.
    </p>

    <theorem xml:id="ham-sandwich">
      <title>Ham Sandwich Theorem (Planar Version)</title>
      <statement>
        Given two finite sets of points in the plane (blue and red),
        each with an even number of points and no three collinear,
        there exists a line that simultaneously bisects both sets.
      </statement>
    </theorem>

    <p>
      This theorem implies that even convex cuts can be used strategically.
      Compactness alone does not guarantee fairness.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="efficiency-gap">
    <title>The Efficiency Gap</title>

    <p>
      The Efficiency Gap measures wasted votes.
    </p>

    <definition>
      <term>Wasted Votes</term>
      <statement>
        <ul>
          <li>All votes for the losing candidate.</li>
          <li>All votes beyond 50% for the winning candidate.</li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Efficiency Gap:
    </p>

    <me>
      EG = \frac{\text{Wasted Votes}_A - \text{Wasted Votes}_B}{\text{Total Votes}}
    </me>

    <p>
      Rough guideline: ±8% is considered the acceptable range.
    </p>

    <p>
      In our tiny state:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Proportional plan → small EG (~8%).</li>
      <li>Blue gerrymander → large positive EG (~40%).</li>
      <li>Red gerrymander → large negative EG (~32%).</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Drawbacks:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>Only measures partisan bias.</li>
      <li>False positives/negatives possible.</li>
      <li>Not fully compatible with proportionality.</li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="sampling">
    <title>Sampling the Space of District Plans</title>

    <p>
      Instead of scoring a single map, we:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>Generate many valid district plans (subject to constraints).</li>
      <li>Compute election outcomes for each plan.</li>
      <li>Compare the proposed plan to the distribution.</li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      By the Central Limit Theorem, outcomes across many plans
      tend to form a normal distribution.
    </p>

    <p>
      A plan that lies far in the tail is statistically suspicious.
    </p>

    <p>
      This approach can test racial, socioeconomic, and partisan hypotheses.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="whos-who">
    <title>Who’s Who in Mathematical Districting</title>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Mira Bernstein &amp; Moon Duchin (Tufts) — convex geometry and districting.
      </li>
      <li>
        Jonathan Mattingly (Duke) — quantifying gerrymandering.
      </li>
      <li>
        Princeton Gerrymandering Project — sampling software.
      </li>
      <li>
        AMS Capital Currents Blog (Karen Saxe).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </subsection>


  <subsection xml:id="closing">
    <title>Closing Question</title>

    <p>
      What should fairness mean in representative democracy?
    </p>

    <p>
      Mathematics cannot answer that normative question —
      but it can reveal when something is statistically extreme.
    </p>
  </subsection>

</section>
</article>
</pretext>